// Generated by scripts/restructure-by-construct.
// Per-construct DSL layout: every <kind>s.memql file in a domain
// holds every construct of that kind in the domain. The companion
// files cover the other construct kinds.

use work.builtins.{ cancelComputerUseRuns }

// killSwitchCancelsComputerUseRuns replaces killSwitchSuspendsRunningPlans,
// which was deleted with the Plan concepts in memql#5053 (memql#5066).
//
// ONE OF THE THREE FINDINGS BEHIND THAT DELETION WAS WRONG, and it is the one
// that made the deletion look free. It said the automation "never fired",
// citing its query's own doc comment: `args.event.payload.id ?? ""` reached
// argument validation as an unevaluated CoalesceExpr (memql#2870). That was
// true until 8ef364cd7 fixed it on 2026-07-27, six weeks before the deletion.
// The comment survived its own fix and was read as current.
// component/memql/nested_call_arg_eval_test.go drives the sibling cases
// through the real resolver and they pass on main, so this is a RESTORATION of
// a control that was live, not the construction of one that never was.
//
// The other two findings stand, and shaped what replaced it:
//
//   - its predicate was `computerUseScope != null`, a Plan field with no run
//     equivalent. The successor does not add one: v1:worker:invocation.runId
//     already names the run a call was made inside, so "runs actually using
//     the worker" is a fact the dispatcher records per call. It is STRICTER
//     than the old predicate -- a scope was permission, an invocation is use.
//   - the ENFORCED control is untouched and is elsewhere:
//     integrations/agent/worker/dispatch.go reads the flag before EVERY
//     dispatch and returns deny with `kill_switch_engaged`, failing closed on
//     a lookup error. That gate stops every NEW call the moment the switch
//     flips. This automation is only the in-flight half.
//
// THE FILTER IS THE GATE, not a hint. `node.updated` on v1:identity:user fires
// for every write to a user row; the automation must only act on the one that
// turned computer use OFF.

/// When a user turns computer use off, ask their in-flight runs that used a machine to stop.
/// Cancellation is REQUESTED: a run notices at its next step boundary, so a step already in flight
/// finishes and is journaled rather than being abandoned mid-effect. The builtin is gated on
/// INTERNAL ORIGIN, which this automation carries because it is tree-loaded AND its payload is the
/// engine's own -- the pair memql#2888 requires, and the one its predecessor was attacked through.
@trigger(event="node.updated", concept="v1:identity:user", partition="*")
@filter(payload.preferences.computerUseEnabled == false)
automation killSwitchCancelsComputerUseRuns {
  // The trigger payload binds INTO this contract at fire time, and the field
  // is then read BARE. Its predecessor reached for `args.event.payload.id ??
  // ""` instead, which is the retired dotted form -- and the coalesce inside
  // it is what memql#2870 could not evaluate. Declaring the field is what
  // makes the id a checked input rather than a path walked at runtime.
  args {
    id any
  }

  step stop {
    builtin cancelComputerUseRuns (
      ownerUserId: id
    )
  }
}

// workerInvocationRetentionSweep
// ===============================
//
// Daily 02:30 UTC sweep. Soft-deletes worker-invocation rows older
// than WORKER_INVOCATION_RETENTION_DAYS (default 90). Per-user
// invocation queries already filter on the isNotDeleted trait (see
// dsl/worker/queries.memql) so the rows disappear from UIs the moment
// the soft-delete lands.
//
// Different from auditEventRetentionSweep on the identity side
// (default 365) because per-call telemetry is high-volume and
// security signals are not. Operators tune via the
// WORKER_INVOCATION_RETENTION_DAYS env var.
//
// MemQL parser limitations:
//   - The filter side of expiredWorkerInvocations can't push
//     the duration math into the query (no `>` operator on
//     datetime). Per-row gating uses
//       addDuration(item.createdAt, "P{retention}D") < now
/// Daily 02:30 UTC sweep: soft-delete worker-invocation rows past WORKER_INVOCATION_RETENTION_DAYS.
/// decide (pure) returns the candidate rows; window reads the retention var; the forEach apply
/// gates each row on createdAt + P{N}D < now (#2256/#2254 evaluator fix) and soft-deletes it.
@trigger(schedule="0 30 2 * * *")
automation workerInvocationRetentionSweep {
  step decide {
    logic workerInvocationRetentionSweep ( event: event )
  }
  step apply {
    forEach item in decide.result {
      softDeleteWorkerInvocation {
        invocationId: item.id
      }
    }
  }
}

// workerModelPullStaleSweep
// =========================
//
// Every two minutes, fail the model pulls nobody is driving (epic memql#5103).
//
// TWO MINUTES, not nightly, and the cadence is the point. A retention sweep can
// run at 02:30 because nobody is waiting on it; this one closes a spinner
// somebody is looking at right now, so its period is the longest a person will
// stare at a bar that is never going to move.
//
// It runs on the CRON LEADER, so exactly one replica sweeps -- and the rows it
// closes belong to whatever replica has gone, which is why it cannot be the
// claiming replica's own job.
//
// It is on the maintenance-principal list (component/auth/maintenance_actor.go)
// because openModelPulls reads across owners BY NATURE: a sweep for abandoned
// work cannot know whose work was abandoned before it looks. Without the
// principal the read returns zero rows and no error, and a cluster full of
// stuck pulls sweeps nothing every two minutes, forever, in silence.
/// Every two minutes: fail the model pulls no replica is driving. decide (pure) returns the
/// candidates, judged against two separate cutoffs; the forEach closes each one.
///
/// ONE SENTENCE FOR BOTH SHAPES OF ABANDONMENT, deliberately. The decide step distinguishes them
/// -- a `requested` row nobody claimed from a `running` row that stopped reporting -- but a
/// forEach writes one literal, so a message naming a single cause would be wrong half the time.
/// The remedy is identical either way, which is what the sentence says instead.
@trigger(schedule="0 */2 * * * *")
automation workerModelPullStaleSweep {
  step decide {
    logic workerModelPullStaleSweep ( event: event )
  }
  step apply {
    forEach item in decide.result {
      finishModelPull {
        pullId:       item.id,
        status:       "failed",
        statusLine:   "",
        errorMessage: "No agent replica is driving this pull -- either the node holding the machine's connection stopped before it began, or it went away mid-download. Whatever was already fetched stays on the machine, so starting the pull again resumes rather than restarts.",
        readvertised: false,
        endedAt:      now
      }
    }
  }
}


// workerModelProbeStaleSweep
// ==========================
//
// Every two minutes, fail the model probes nobody is driving (epic memql#5146).
//
// The pull sweep's cadence for the pull sweep's reason: this closes a counter
// somebody is looking at right now, so its period is the longest a person will
// stare at a number that is never going to move.
//
// It runs on the CRON LEADER, so exactly one replica sweeps -- and the rows it
// closes belong to whatever replica has gone, which is why it cannot be the
// claiming replica's own job.
//
// It is on the maintenance-principal list (component/auth/maintenance_actor.go)
// because openModelProbes reads across owners BY NATURE: a sweep for abandoned
// work cannot know whose work was abandoned before it looks. Without the
// principal the read returns zero rows and no error, and a cluster full of
// stuck probes sweeps nothing every two minutes, forever, in silence.

/// Every two minutes: fail the model probes no replica is driving. decide (pure) returns the
/// candidates, judged against two separate cutoffs; the forEach closes each one.
///
/// ONE SENTENCE FOR BOTH SHAPES OF ABANDONMENT, deliberately -- a forEach writes one literal, so a
/// message naming a single cause would be wrong half the time, and the remedy is identical either
/// way. The sentence says what a person actually needs: nothing was measured, so nothing was
/// recorded, and running it again costs only the time.
@trigger(schedule="0 */2 * * * *")
automation workerModelProbeStaleSweep {
  step decide {
    logic workerModelProbeStaleSweep ( event: event )
  }
  step apply {
    forEach item in decide.result {
      finishModelProbe {
        probeId:       item.id,
        status:        "failed",
        measurementId: "",
        errorMessage:  "No agent replica is driving this probe -- either the node holding the machine's connection stopped before it began, or it went away mid-suite. Nothing was measured, so nothing was recorded; running it again costs only the time.",
        endedAt:       now
      }
    }
  }
}
